IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `uc-core`

-- The Universal Credit Regulations 2013 (S.I. 2013/376), Part 6, Chapter 2 (EARNED INCOME): self-employed earnings,
-- regulations 57, 57A, 58 and 59.
-- Source: ../../registers/source-bundle/uksi-2013-376.txt (lines 970-1027), a plain-text rendering made by a converter script; it is not the official text.
-- Target: the stable build 1.6.98. Money is a NUMBER of pounds; a calculated amount is rounded once, at the end, with the keel's
-- `core — an amount rounded to the nearest penny` (regulation 6(1)). A step of regulation 57(2) is a rule of its own and does
-- not round; only the amount that regulation 57(2) finally states is rounded.

§ `Part 6, Chapter 2 -- Self-employed earnings`

§§ `Regulation 57(1) -- Scope (inert)`
-- src:970 | 57 Self-employed earnings
-- src:971 | (1) This regulation applies for the purpose of calculating earned income that is not employed earnings and is derived from carrying on a trade, profession or vocation (“self-employed earnings”).
-- INERT: paragraph (1) says what the regulation is for (earned income that is not employed earnings and is derived from carrying on
-- a trade, profession or vocation). The rules below are called for that income only.

§§ `Regulation 57(4) and (5) -- Receipts`

-- Paragraph (5): where the purchase of an asset has been deducted as an expense in an earlier assessment period and, in a later one,
-- the asset is sold or ceases to be used for the trade, the proceeds of sale (or, if it ceases to be used, the amount that would be
-- received if it were sold at its current market value) are a receipt of the later period.
-- src:991 | (5) Where the purchase of an asset has been deducted as an expense in any assessment period and, in a subsequent assessment period, the asset is sold or ceases to be used for the purposes of a trade, profession or vocation carried on by the person, the proceeds of sale (or, as the case may be, the amount that would be received for the asset if it were sold at its current market value) are to be treated as a receipt in that subsequent assessment period.
@ref reg 57(5) (src:991)
DECLARE `Asset the purchase of which was deducted as an expense` HAS
    `the purchase was deducted as an expense in an earlier assessment period`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the asset has been sold`                                                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the asset has ceased to be used for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `proceeds of sale of the asset`                                             IS A NUMBER
    `amount that would be received for the asset if it were sold at its current market value`  IS A NUMBER

-- src:991 | (5) Where the purchase of an asset has been deducted as an expense in any assessment period and, in a subsequent assessment period, the asset is sold or ceases to be used for the purposes of a trade, profession or vocation carried on by the person, the proceeds of sale (or, as the case may be, the amount that would be received for the asset if it were sold at its current market value) are to be treated as a receipt in that subsequent assessment period.
@ref reg 57(5) (src:991)
GIVEN asset IS AN `Asset the purchase of which was deducted as an expense`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 57(5) — the amount treated as a receipt` asset MEANS
    IF asset's `the purchase was deducted as an expense in an earlier assessment period`
    THEN (IF asset's `the asset has been sold`
          THEN asset's `proceeds of sale of the asset`
          ELSE (IF asset's `the asset has ceased to be used for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`
                THEN asset's `amount that would be received for the asset if it were sold at its current market value`
                ELSE 0))
    ELSE 0

-- src:971 | (1) This regulation applies for the purpose of calculating earned income that is not employed earnings and is derived from carrying on a trade, profession or vocation (“self-employed earnings”).
-- src:972 | (2) A person’s self-employed earnings in respect of an assessment period are to be calculated as follows.
-- src:973 | Step 1
-- src:974 | Calculate the amount of the person’s profit or loss in respect of each trade, profession or vocation carried on by the person by—
-- src:975 | (a) taking the actual receipts in that assessment period; and
-- src:976 | (b) deducting any amounts allowed as expenses under regulation 58 or 59.
-- src:977 | Where a trade, profession or vocation is carried on in a partnership, take the amount of the profit or loss attributable to the person’s share in the partnership.
@ref reg 57(2) Step 1 (src:971-977)
DECLARE `Trade, profession or vocation carried on in an assessment period` HAS
    `actual receipts in the assessment period`                                                 IS A NUMBER
    `receipts in kind (their value)`                                                           IS A NUMBER
    `any refund or repayment of income tax, value added tax or national insurance contributions relating to the trade, profession or vocation`  IS A NUMBER
    `assets treated as receipts of the assessment period`                                      IS A LIST OF `Asset the purchase of which was deducted as an expense`
    `amounts allowed as expenses under regulation 58 or 59`                                    IS A NUMBER
    `share of the profit or loss attributable to the person, where the trade, profession or vocation is carried on in a partnership`  IS A MAYBE NUMBER

-- src:990 | (4) The receipts referred to in paragraph (2) include receipts in kind and any refund or repayment of income tax, value added tax or national insurance contributions relating to the trade, profession or vocation.
@ref reg 57(4) (src:990)
GIVEN trade IS A `Trade, profession or vocation carried on in an assessment period`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 57(4) — the receipts` trade MEANS
        (trade's `actual receipts in the assessment period`)
    PLUS (trade's `receipts in kind (their value)`)
    PLUS (trade's `any refund or repayment of income tax, value added tax or national insurance contributions relating to the trade, profession or vocation`)
    PLUS (sum (map (GIVEN asset YIELD `reg 57(5) — the amount treated as a receipt` asset) (trade's `assets treated as receipts of the assessment period`)))

§§ `Regulation 57(2) -- Self-employed earnings: the five steps`

-- Step 1: the profit or loss from each trade: the actual receipts less the expenses allowed under regulations 58 and 59. Where the
-- trade is carried on in a partnership, the profit or loss attributable to the person's share is taken (a fraction of the whole).
-- src:971 | (1) This regulation applies for the purpose of calculating earned income that is not employed earnings and is derived from carrying on a trade, profession or vocation (“self-employed earnings”).
-- src:972 | (2) A person’s self-employed earnings in respect of an assessment period are to be calculated as follows.
-- src:973 | Step 1
-- src:974 | Calculate the amount of the person’s profit or loss in respect of each trade, profession or vocation carried on by the person by—
-- src:975 | (a) taking the actual receipts in that assessment period; and
-- src:976 | (b) deducting any amounts allowed as expenses under regulation 58 or 59.
-- src:977 | Where a trade, profession or vocation is carried on in a partnership, take the amount of the profit or loss attributable to the person’s share in the partnership.
@ref reg 57(2) Step 1 (src:971-977)
GIVEN trade IS A `Trade, profession or vocation carried on in an assessment period`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 57(2) Step 1 — the profit or loss from a trade, profession or vocation` trade MEANS
    CONSIDER trade's `share of the profit or loss attributable to the person, where the trade, profession or vocation is carried on in a partnership`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN (`reg 57(4) — the receipts` trade) MINUS (trade's `amounts allowed as expenses under regulation 58 or 59`)
    WHEN JUST share THEN share TIMES ((`reg 57(4) — the receipts` trade) MINUS (trade's `amounts allowed as expenses under regulation 58 or 59`))

-- Step 2: add together the Step 1 amounts where more than one trade is carried on.
-- src:978 | Step 2
-- src:979 | If the person has carried on more than one trade, profession or vocation in the assessment period, add together the amounts resulting from step 1 in respect of each trade, profession or vocation.
@ref reg 57(2) Step 2 (src:978-979)
GIVEN trades IS A LIST OF `Trade, profession or vocation carried on in an assessment period`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 57(2) Step 2 — the profits and losses added together` trades MEANS
    sum (map (GIVEN trade YIELD `reg 57(2) Step 1 — the profit or loss from a trade, profession or vocation` trade) trades)

-- src:971 | (1) This regulation applies for the purpose of calculating earned income that is not employed earnings and is derived from carrying on a trade, profession or vocation (“self-employed earnings”).
-- src:972 | (2) A person’s self-employed earnings in respect of an assessment period are to be calculated as follows.
-- src:973 | Step 1
-- src:974 | Calculate the amount of the person’s profit or loss in respect of each trade, profession or vocation carried on by the person by—
-- src:975 | (a) taking the actual receipts in that assessment period; and
-- src:976 | (b) deducting any amounts allowed as expenses under regulation 58 or 59.
-- src:977 | Where a trade, profession or vocation is carried on in a partnership, take the amount of the profit or loss attributable to the person’s share in the partnership.
-- src:978 | Step 2
-- src:979 | If the person has carried on more than one trade, profession or vocation in the assessment period, add together the amounts resulting from step 1 in respect of each trade, profession or vocation.
-- src:980 | Step 3
-- src:981 | Deduct from the amount resulting from step 1 or (if applicable) step 2 any payment made by the person to HMRC in the assessment period by way of national insurance contributions or income tax in respect of any trade, profession or vocation carried on by the person.
-- src:982 | If the amount resulting from steps 1 to 3 is nil or a negative amount, the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings in respect of the assessment period is nil (and ignore the following steps).
-- src:983 | Step 4
-- src:984 | If the amount resulting from step 3 is greater than nil, deduct from that amount any relievable pension contributions made by the person in the assessment period (unless a deduction has been made in respect of those contributions in calculating the person’s employed earnings).
-- src:985 | If the amount resulting from this step is nil or a negative amount, the person’s self-employed earnings in respect of the assessment period are nil (and ignore the following step).
-- src:986 | Step 5
-- src:987 | If the amount resulting from step 4 is greater than nil, deduct from that amount any unused losses (see regulation 57A), taking the oldest first.
-- src:988 | If the amount resulting from this step is greater than nil, that is the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings for the assessment period.
-- src:989 | If the amount resulting from this step is nil or a negative amount, the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings in respect of the assessment period is nil.
@ref reg 57(2) (src:971-989)
DECLARE `Self-employed earnings considered under regulation 57` HAS
    `trades, professions or vocations carried on by the person in the assessment period`       IS A LIST OF `Trade, profession or vocation carried on in an assessment period`
    `payments made by the person to HMRC in the assessment period by way of national insurance contributions or income tax in respect of any trade, profession or vocation`  IS A NUMBER
    `relievable pension contributions made by the person in the assessment period`            IS A NUMBER
    `a deduction has been made in respect of those contributions in calculating the person's employed earnings`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `unused losses, oldest first`                                                              IS A LIST OF NUMBER

-- Step 3: deduct payments to HMRC in the assessment period by way of national insurance contributions or income tax.
-- src:980 | Step 3
-- src:981 | Deduct from the amount resulting from step 1 or (if applicable) step 2 any payment made by the person to HMRC in the assessment period by way of national insurance contributions or income tax in respect of any trade, profession or vocation carried on by the person.
@ref reg 57(2) Step 3 (src:980-981)
GIVEN earnings IS A `Self-employed earnings considered under regulation 57`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 57(2) Step 3 — the amount after payments to HMRC` earnings MEANS
    (`reg 57(2) Step 2 — the profits and losses added together` (earnings's `trades, professions or vocations carried on by the person in the assessment period`))
    MINUS (earnings's `payments made by the person to HMRC in the assessment period by way of national insurance contributions or income tax in respect of any trade, profession or vocation`)

-- Step 4: if Step 3 is greater than nil, deduct relievable pension contributions made in the assessment period, unless a deduction
-- has been made for them in calculating employed earnings.
-- src:983 | Step 4
-- src:984 | If the amount resulting from step 3 is greater than nil, deduct from that amount any relievable pension contributions made by the person in the assessment period (unless a deduction has been made in respect of those contributions in calculating the person’s employed earnings).
@ref reg 57(2) Step 4 (src:983-984)
GIVEN earnings IS A `Self-employed earnings considered under regulation 57`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 57(2) Step 4 — the amount after relievable pension contributions` earnings MEANS
    (`reg 57(2) Step 3 — the amount after payments to HMRC` earnings)
    MINUS (IF earnings's `a deduction has been made in respect of those contributions in calculating the person's employed earnings`
           THEN 0
           ELSE earnings's `relievable pension contributions made by the person in the assessment period`)

-- Step 5: if Step 4 is greater than nil, deduct unused losses, oldest first. A loss is deducted whole or, if the amount left runs
-- out, in part; what remains of a part-deducted loss stays unused (regulation 57A(2): a loss is extinguished only if no amount
-- of it remains).
-- src:986 | Step 5
-- src:987 | If the amount resulting from step 4 is greater than nil, deduct from that amount any unused losses (see regulation 57A), taking the oldest first.
-- src:988 | If the amount resulting from this step is greater than nil, that is the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings for the assessment period.
-- src:989 | If the amount resulting from this step is nil or a negative amount, the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings in respect of the assessment period is nil.
@ref reg 57(2) Step 5 (src:986-989)
GIVEN remaining IS A NUMBER
      losses IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`reg 57(2) Step 5 — the unused losses left after deducting from an amount` remaining losses MEANS
    CONSIDER losses
    WHEN EMPTY THEN EMPTY
    WHEN loss FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        IF remaining AT MOST 0
        THEN losses
        ELSE (IF loss AT MOST remaining
              THEN `reg 57(2) Step 5 — the unused losses left after deducting from an amount` (remaining MINUS loss) rest
              ELSE (loss MINUS remaining) FOLLOWED BY rest)

-- src:986 | Step 5
-- src:987 | If the amount resulting from step 4 is greater than nil, deduct from that amount any unused losses (see regulation 57A), taking the oldest first.
-- src:988 | If the amount resulting from this step is greater than nil, that is the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings for the assessment period.
-- src:989 | If the amount resulting from this step is nil or a negative amount, the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings in respect of the assessment period is nil.
@ref reg 57(2) Step 5 (src:986-989)
GIVEN earnings IS A `Self-employed earnings considered under regulation 57`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 57(2) Step 5 — the amount after unused losses` earnings MEANS
    max 0 ((`reg 57(2) Step 4 — the amount after relievable pension contributions` earnings) MINUS (sum (earnings's `unused losses, oldest first`)))

-- The five steps together. If Steps 1 to 3 give nil or less, the earnings are nil and the later steps are ignored; if Step 4 gives
-- nil or less, the earnings are nil and Step 5 is ignored.
-- src:971 | (1) This regulation applies for the purpose of calculating earned income that is not employed earnings and is derived from carrying on a trade, profession or vocation (“self-employed earnings”).
-- src:972 | (2) A person’s self-employed earnings in respect of an assessment period are to be calculated as follows.
-- src:973 | Step 1
-- src:974 | Calculate the amount of the person’s profit or loss in respect of each trade, profession or vocation carried on by the person by—
-- src:975 | (a) taking the actual receipts in that assessment period; and
-- src:976 | (b) deducting any amounts allowed as expenses under regulation 58 or 59.
-- src:977 | Where a trade, profession or vocation is carried on in a partnership, take the amount of the profit or loss attributable to the person’s share in the partnership.
-- src:978 | Step 2
-- src:979 | If the person has carried on more than one trade, profession or vocation in the assessment period, add together the amounts resulting from step 1 in respect of each trade, profession or vocation.
-- src:980 | Step 3
-- src:981 | Deduct from the amount resulting from step 1 or (if applicable) step 2 any payment made by the person to HMRC in the assessment period by way of national insurance contributions or income tax in respect of any trade, profession or vocation carried on by the person.
-- src:982 | If the amount resulting from steps 1 to 3 is nil or a negative amount, the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings in respect of the assessment period is nil (and ignore the following steps).
-- src:983 | Step 4
-- src:984 | If the amount resulting from step 3 is greater than nil, deduct from that amount any relievable pension contributions made by the person in the assessment period (unless a deduction has been made in respect of those contributions in calculating the person’s employed earnings).
-- src:985 | If the amount resulting from this step is nil or a negative amount, the person’s self-employed earnings in respect of the assessment period are nil (and ignore the following step).
-- src:986 | Step 5
-- src:987 | If the amount resulting from step 4 is greater than nil, deduct from that amount any unused losses (see regulation 57A), taking the oldest first.
-- src:988 | If the amount resulting from this step is greater than nil, that is the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings for the assessment period.
-- src:989 | If the amount resulting from this step is nil or a negative amount, the amount of the person’s self-employed earnings in respect of the assessment period is nil.
@ref reg 57(2) (src:971-989)
GIVEN earnings IS A `Self-employed earnings considered under regulation 57`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 57(2) — the self-employed earnings for an assessment period` earnings MEANS
    IF (`reg 57(2) Step 3 — the amount after payments to HMRC` earnings) AT MOST 0
    THEN 0
    ELSE (IF (`reg 57(2) Step 4 — the amount after relievable pension contributions` earnings) AT MOST 0
          THEN 0
          ELSE `core — an amount rounded to the nearest penny` (`reg 57(2) Step 5 — the amount after unused losses` earnings))

§§ `Regulation 57A -- Unused losses`

-- Paragraph (1)(a): a loss is a negative amount from Steps 1 to 3 in an earlier assessment period. The loss that an assessment
-- period adds is that amount, made positive; there is none if Steps 1 to 3 give nil or more.
-- src:992 | 57A Unused losses
-- src:993 | (1) For the purposes of regulation 57(2), a person has an unused loss if—
-- src:994 | (a) in calculating the person’s self-employed earnings for any of the previous assessment periods, the amount resulting from steps 1 to 3 in regulation 57(2) was a negative amount (a “loss”); and
-- src:995 | (b) the loss has not been extinguished in a subsequent assessment period.
@ref reg 57A(1) (src:992-995)
GIVEN earnings IS A `Self-employed earnings considered under regulation 57`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 57A(1)(a) — the loss for the assessment period` earnings MEANS
    IF (`reg 57(2) Step 3 — the amount after payments to HMRC` earnings) LESS THAN 0
    THEN `core — an amount rounded to the nearest penny` (0 MINUS (`reg 57(2) Step 3 — the amount after payments to HMRC` earnings))
    ELSE 0

-- Paragraphs (1)(b) and (2): the unused losses the next assessment period starts with. Where the Steps 1 to 3 amount was a loss, no
-- Step 5 deduction is made and the new loss goes to the end of the list (newest last). Where Step 4 gave nil or less, Step 5 is
-- ignored and the list is unchanged. Otherwise Step 5 extinguishes losses oldest first.
-- src:992 | 57A Unused losses
-- src:993 | (1) For the purposes of regulation 57(2), a person has an unused loss if—
-- src:994 | (a) in calculating the person’s self-employed earnings for any of the previous assessment periods, the amount resulting from steps 1 to 3 in regulation 57(2) was a negative amount (a “loss”); and
-- src:995 | (b) the loss has not been extinguished in a subsequent assessment period.
-- src:996 | (2) For the purposes of paragraph (1)(b) a loss is extinguished if no amount of that loss remains after it has been deducted at step 5 in regulation 57(2).
@ref reg 57A(1) and (2) (src:992-996)
GIVEN earnings IS A `Self-employed earnings considered under regulation 57`
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`reg 57A — the unused losses carried to the next assessment period` earnings MEANS
    IF (`reg 57(2) Step 3 — the amount after payments to HMRC` earnings) AT MOST 0
    THEN (IF (`reg 57A(1)(a) — the loss for the assessment period` earnings) GREATER THAN 0
          THEN append (earnings's `unused losses, oldest first`) (LIST `reg 57A(1)(a) — the loss for the assessment period` earnings)
          ELSE earnings's `unused losses, oldest first`)
    ELSE (IF (`reg 57(2) Step 4 — the amount after relievable pension contributions` earnings) AT MOST 0
          THEN earnings's `unused losses, oldest first`
          ELSE `reg 57(2) Step 5 — the unused losses left after deducting from an amount` (`reg 57(2) Step 4 — the amount after relievable pension contributions` earnings) (earnings's `unused losses, oldest first`))

-- Paragraph (3): where the person had a previous award whose last day of entitlement fell within the 6 months preceding the first day
-- of entitlement in respect of the new award, the Secretary of State may, if the person provides the information required, treat the
-- previous award's assessment periods and the months in between for which a claim has been made as assessment periods under the
-- current award. The 6 months run back from the first day of the new entitlement; the last day of entitlement must come before it.
-- src:997 | (3) Where a person was entitled to a previous award of universal credit and the last day of entitlement in respect of that award fell within the 6 months preceding the first day of entitlement in respect of the new award, the Secretary of State may, for the purposes of this regulation (provided the person provides such information as the Secretary of State requires), treat—
-- src:998 | (a) the assessment periods under the previous award; and
-- src:999 | (b) any months between that award and the current award in respect of which a claim has been made,
-- src:1000 | as assessment periods under the current award.
@ref reg 57A(3) (src:997-1000)
GIVEN `last day of entitlement in respect of the previous award` IS A DATE
      `first day of entitlement in respect of the new award` IS A DATE
      `the person provides the information the Secretary of State requires` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Secretary of State treats those assessment periods and months as assessment periods under the current award` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reg 57A(3) — the previous assessment periods count as assessment periods under the current award` `last day of entitlement in respect of the previous award` `first day of entitlement in respect of the new award` `the person provides the information the Secretary of State requires` `the Secretary of State treats those assessment periods and months as assessment periods under the current award` MEANS
        (`last day of entitlement in respect of the previous award` LESS THAN `first day of entitlement in respect of the new award`)
    AND (`first day of entitlement in respect of the new award` AT MOST (`core — the date reached by adding a time limit to a date` `last day of entitlement in respect of the previous award` (`Time limit` WITH quantity IS 6, unit IS months)))
    AND `the person provides the information the Secretary of State requires`
    AND `the Secretary of State treats those assessment periods and months as assessment periods under the current award`

§§ `Regulation 58 -- Permitted expenses`

-- Paragraph (3) lists payments for which no deduction may be made. (3)(b) is a run of dots in this text. Paragraph (3A) caps a
-- deduction for a payment of interest on a loan taken out for the trade at 41 pounds. A payment for the acquisition or use of a
-- car, or (where the person takes the regulation 59 deduction instead) a vehicle or the person's home, is dealt with by regulation
-- 59 and is not deducted here. A payment for premises used mainly for the trade and also occupied for personal use is allowed here
-- at the amount that would be allowed if the premises were used wholly and exclusively for the trade (regulation 59(4)); the
-- reduction by the personal-use amount is made in the total.
-- src:1001 | 58 Permitted expenses
-- src:1002 | (1) The deductions allowed in the calculation of self-employed earnings are amounts paid in the assessment period in respect of—
-- src:1003 | (a) expenses that have been wholly and exclusively incurred for purposes of the trade, profession or vocation; or
-- src:1004 | (b) in the case of expenses that have been incurred for more than one purpose, an identifiable part or proportion that has been wholly and exclusively incurred for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation,
-- src:1005 | excluding any expenses that were incurred unreasonably.
-- src:1006 | (2) Payments deducted under paragraph (1) may include value added tax.
-- src:1007 | (3) No deduction may be made for payments in respect of—
-- src:1008 | (a) expenditure on non-depreciating assets (including property, shares or other assets held for investment purposes);
-- src:1009 | (b) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:1010 | (c) repayment of capital . . . in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation;
-- src:1011 | (d) expenses for business entertainment.
-- src:1012 | (3A) A deduction for a payment of interest in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation may not exceed £41.
-- src:1013 | (4) This regulation is subject to regulation 59.
@ref reg 58 (src:1001-1013)
DECLARE `Kind of payment for a trade, profession or vocation` IS ONE OF
    `an ordinary payment for the trade, profession or vocation`
    `expenditure on a non-depreciating asset (including property, shares or other assets held for investment purposes)`
    `repayment of capital in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`
    `expenses for business entertainment`
    `a payment of interest in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`
    `acquisition or use of a car`
    `acquisition or use of a motor vehicle that is not a car`
    `use of accommodation occupied as the person's home`
    `use of premises used mainly for the trade, profession or vocation and also occupied for personal use`

-- src:1001 | 58 Permitted expenses
-- src:1002 | (1) The deductions allowed in the calculation of self-employed earnings are amounts paid in the assessment period in respect of—
-- src:1003 | (a) expenses that have been wholly and exclusively incurred for purposes of the trade, profession or vocation; or
-- src:1004 | (b) in the case of expenses that have been incurred for more than one purpose, an identifiable part or proportion that has been wholly and exclusively incurred for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation,
-- src:1005 | excluding any expenses that were incurred unreasonably.
-- src:1006 | (2) Payments deducted under paragraph (1) may include value added tax.
-- src:1007 | (3) No deduction may be made for payments in respect of—
-- src:1008 | (a) expenditure on non-depreciating assets (including property, shares or other assets held for investment purposes);
-- src:1009 | (b) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:1010 | (c) repayment of capital . . . in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation;
-- src:1011 | (d) expenses for business entertainment.
-- src:1012 | (3A) A deduction for a payment of interest in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation may not exceed £41.
-- src:1013 | (4) This regulation is subject to regulation 59.
@ref reg 58 (src:1001-1013)
DECLARE `Payment made in the assessment period for a trade, profession or vocation` HAS
    `kind of payment`                                                                          IS A `Kind of payment for a trade, profession or vocation`
    `amount paid in the assessment period (which may include value added tax)`                 IS A NUMBER
    `wholly and exclusively incurred for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `incurred for more than one purpose`                                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `identifiable proportion that has been wholly and exclusively incurred for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`  IS A MAYBE NUMBER
    `incurred unreasonably`                                                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person takes the deduction under regulation 59 instead of the actual expense`        IS A BOOLEAN

-- Paragraph (1): the amount paid is allowed if wholly and exclusively incurred for the trade; if incurred for more than one purpose,
-- the identifiable part or proportion that was wholly and exclusively incurred; but not an expense incurred unreasonably.
-- src:1002 | (1) The deductions allowed in the calculation of self-employed earnings are amounts paid in the assessment period in respect of—
-- src:1003 | (a) expenses that have been wholly and exclusively incurred for purposes of the trade, profession or vocation; or
-- src:1004 | (b) in the case of expenses that have been incurred for more than one purpose, an identifiable part or proportion that has been wholly and exclusively incurred for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation,
-- src:1005 | excluding any expenses that were incurred unreasonably.
-- src:1006 | (2) Payments deducted under paragraph (1) may include value added tax.
@ref reg 58(1) and (2) (src:1002-1006)
GIVEN payment IS A `Payment made in the assessment period for a trade, profession or vocation`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 58(1) — the part of a payment that is allowed` payment MEANS
    IF payment's `incurred unreasonably`
    THEN 0
    ELSE (IF payment's `wholly and exclusively incurred for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`
          THEN payment's `amount paid in the assessment period (which may include value added tax)`
          ELSE (IF payment's `incurred for more than one purpose`
                THEN (CONSIDER payment's `identifiable proportion that has been wholly and exclusively incurred for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`
                      WHEN JUST proportion THEN proportion TIMES (payment's `amount paid in the assessment period (which may include value added tax)`)
                      WHEN NOTHING THEN 0)
                ELSE 0))

-- src:1007 | (3) No deduction may be made for payments in respect of—
-- src:1008 | (a) expenditure on non-depreciating assets (including property, shares or other assets held for investment purposes);
-- src:1009 | (b) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:1010 | (c) repayment of capital . . . in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation;
-- src:1011 | (d) expenses for business entertainment.
-- src:1012 | (3A) A deduction for a payment of interest in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation may not exceed £41.
-- src:1013 | (4) This regulation is subject to regulation 59.
@ref reg 58(3), (3A) and (4) (src:1007-1013)
GIVEN payment IS A `Payment made in the assessment period for a trade, profession or vocation`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 58 — the deduction allowed for a payment` payment MEANS
    CONSIDER payment's `kind of payment`
    WHEN `an ordinary payment for the trade, profession or vocation` THEN `reg 58(1) — the part of a payment that is allowed` payment
    WHEN `expenditure on a non-depreciating asset (including property, shares or other assets held for investment purposes)` THEN 0
    WHEN `repayment of capital in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation` THEN 0
    WHEN `expenses for business entertainment` THEN 0
    WHEN `a payment of interest in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation` THEN min 41 (`reg 58(1) — the part of a payment that is allowed` payment)
    WHEN `acquisition or use of a car` THEN 0
    WHEN `acquisition or use of a motor vehicle that is not a car` THEN
        IF payment's `the person takes the deduction under regulation 59 instead of the actual expense`
        THEN 0
        ELSE `reg 58(1) — the part of a payment that is allowed` payment
    WHEN `use of accommodation occupied as the person's home` THEN
        IF payment's `the person takes the deduction under regulation 59 instead of the actual expense`
        THEN 0
        ELSE `reg 58(1) — the part of a payment that is allowed` payment
    WHEN `use of premises used mainly for the trade, profession or vocation and also occupied for personal use` THEN
        IF payment's `incurred unreasonably` THEN 0 ELSE payment's `amount paid in the assessment period (which may include value added tax)`

§§ `Regulation 59(1) -- Flat rate deductions: purpose (inert)`
-- src:1014 | 59 Flat rate deductions for mileage and use of home and adjustment for personal use of business premises
-- src:1015 | (1) This regulation provides for alternatives to the deductions that would otherwise be allowed under regulation 58.
-- INERT: a statement that the regulation provides alternatives to the deductions of regulation 58; the alternatives are paragraphs
-- (2) to (4), encoded below.

§§ `Regulation 59 -- Flat rate deductions for mileage and use of home, and adjustment for personal use of business premises`

-- Paragraph (2): 45 pence per mile for the first 833 miles and 25 pence per mile thereafter, in a car, van or other motor vehicle
-- (apart from a motorcycle); 24 pence per mile on a motorcycle. The mileage is that "covered on journeys undertaken in the
-- assessment period for the purposes of the trade" (fork F-S5: the 833 miles are read as within the assessment period).
-- src:1016 | (2) Instead of a deduction in respect of the actual expenses incurred in relation to the acquisition or use of a motor vehicle, the following deductions are allowed according to the mileage covered on journeys undertaken in the assessment period for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation—
-- src:1017 | (a) in a car, van or other motor vehicle (apart from a motorcycle), 45 pence per mile for the first 833 miles and 25 pence per mile thereafter; and
-- src:1018 | (b) on a motorcycle, 24 pence per mile,
-- src:1019 | and, if the motor vehicle is a car . . . , the only deduction allowed for the acquisition or use of that vehicle is a deduction under this paragraph.
@ref reg 59(2) (src:1016-1019)
GIVEN `miles covered in a car, van or other motor vehicle (apart from a motorcycle) on journeys undertaken in the assessment period for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation` IS A NUMBER
      `miles covered on a motorcycle on journeys undertaken in the assessment period for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 59(2) — the deduction for mileage` `miles covered in a car, van or other motor vehicle (apart from a motorcycle) on journeys undertaken in the assessment period for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation` `miles covered on a motorcycle on journeys undertaken in the assessment period for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation` MEANS
    `core — an amount rounded to the nearest penny`
        (   (0.45 TIMES (min 833 `miles covered in a car, van or other motor vehicle (apart from a motorcycle) on journeys undertaken in the assessment period for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`))
          PLUS (0.25 TIMES (max 0 (`miles covered in a car, van or other motor vehicle (apart from a motorcycle) on journeys undertaken in the assessment period for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation` MINUS 833)))
          PLUS (0.24 TIMES `miles covered on a motorcycle on journeys undertaken in the assessment period for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation`))

-- Paragraph (3): for expenses of using accommodation occupied as the person's home, in place of the actual expenses: 10 pounds for
-- at least 25 and no more than 50 hours on income generating activities in the assessment period, 18 pounds for more than 50 and no
-- more than 100, and 26 pounds for more than 100. The text allows nothing for fewer than 25 hours (fork F-S6).
-- src:1020 | (3) Where a person carrying on a trade, profession or vocation incurs expenses in relation to the use of accommodation occupied as their home, instead of a deduction in respect of the actual expenses, a deduction is allowed according to the number of hours spent in the assessment period on income generating activities related to the trade, profession or vocation as follows—
-- src:1021 | (a) at least 25 hours but no more than 50 hours, £10;
-- src:1022 | (b) more than 50 hours but no more than 100 hours, £18;
-- src:1023 | (c) more than 100 hours, £26.
@ref reg 59(3) (src:1020-1023)
GIVEN `hours spent in the assessment period on income generating activities related to the trade, profession or vocation` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 59(3) — the deduction for use of the person's home` `hours spent in the assessment period on income generating activities related to the trade, profession or vocation` MEANS
    IF `hours spent in the assessment period on income generating activities related to the trade, profession or vocation` GREATER THAN 100
    THEN 26
    ELSE (IF `hours spent in the assessment period on income generating activities related to the trade, profession or vocation` GREATER THAN 50
          THEN 18
          ELSE (IF `hours spent in the assessment period on income generating activities related to the trade, profession or vocation` AT LEAST 25
                THEN 10
                ELSE 0))

-- Paragraph (4): premises used mainly for the trade and also occupied for personal use. The deduction is the amount that would be
-- allowed under regulation 58(1) if the premises were used wholly and exclusively for the trade, reduced by 350 pounds for one
-- person occupying them for personal use, 500 pounds for two, and 650 pounds for three or more. The deduction is not taken below nil
-- (fork F-S7); with nobody occupying them for personal use there is no reduction.
-- src:1024 | (4) Where premises which are used by a person mainly for the purposes of a trade, profession or vocation are also occupied by that person for their personal use, whether alone or with other persons, the deduction allowed for expenses in relation to those premises is the amount that would be allowed under regulation 58(1) if the premises were used wholly and exclusively for purposes of the trade, profession or vocation, but reduced by the following amount according to the number of persons occupying the premises for their personal use—
-- src:1025 | (a) £350 for one person;
-- src:1026 | (b) £500 for two persons;
-- src:1027 | (c) £650 for three or more persons.
@ref reg 59(4) (src:1024-1027)
GIVEN `number of persons occupying the premises for their personal use` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 59(4) — the reduction for personal use of the premises` `number of persons occupying the premises for their personal use` MEANS
    IF `number of persons occupying the premises for their personal use` AT LEAST 3
    THEN 650
    ELSE (IF `number of persons occupying the premises for their personal use` EQUALS 2
          THEN 500
          ELSE (IF `number of persons occupying the premises for their personal use` EQUALS 1
                THEN 350
                ELSE 0))

-- src:1001 | 58 Permitted expenses
-- src:1002 | (1) The deductions allowed in the calculation of self-employed earnings are amounts paid in the assessment period in respect of—
-- src:1003 | (a) expenses that have been wholly and exclusively incurred for purposes of the trade, profession or vocation; or
-- src:1004 | (b) in the case of expenses that have been incurred for more than one purpose, an identifiable part or proportion that has been wholly and exclusively incurred for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation,
-- src:1005 | excluding any expenses that were incurred unreasonably.
-- src:1006 | (2) Payments deducted under paragraph (1) may include value added tax.
-- src:1007 | (3) No deduction may be made for payments in respect of—
-- src:1008 | (a) expenditure on non-depreciating assets (including property, shares or other assets held for investment purposes);
-- src:1009 | (b) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:1010 | (c) repayment of capital . . . in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation;
-- src:1011 | (d) expenses for business entertainment.
-- src:1012 | (3A) A deduction for a payment of interest in relation to a loan taken out for the purposes of the trade, profession or vocation may not exceed £41.
-- src:1013 | (4) This regulation is subject to regulation 59.
-- src:1014 | 59 Flat rate deductions for mileage and use of home and adjustment for personal use of business premises
